// locale.zm -- the `Locale` type, and nothing else.
//
// **The type is here and its constructors are not**, which is the one odd thing about this part and
// is forced rather than chosen: `to_upper(s, locale)` is an overload of a prelude function, so the
// prelude has to be able to name the type -- and the prelude cannot reference a core module (a
// declaration mentioning `locale.Locale` fails with "no module imported under the name 'locale'",
// which is the rule, not an accident). Meanwhile a `Locale` is built from the operating system's
// locale data through a native, which belongs with the rest of that machinery.
//
// So: the type is prelude vocabulary, and `use locale` gives you `locale.of`, `locale.root`,
// `locale.default`, `locale.set_default`, `locale.system` and `locale.name_of` to make and inspect
// one. lib/core/locale.zm carries what a Locale is for and what the host can and cannot answer.
//
// Part of Ziman's prelude: every file in lib/prelude/ is merged bare into every program by the
// loader, so everything here is callable with no `use` and `use prelude` does not resolve. In this
// directory `pub` means what it means anywhere else as of 2026-10-22: **a declaration without it
// cannot be called from outside the prelude.** `internal` reaches the bundled library's own
// package, so lib/ may call one and a program may not, and a bare declaration belongs to the file
// it sits in.

// A locale, held by handle, with the POSIX name it resolved to.
//
// `handle` is the address of the host locale the collector is keeping alive; holding a `Locale` is
// what keeps it open, and the finalizer closes it. `name` is cached here so reading it costs
// nothing -- and so that a caller can see which locale they actually got, an unavailable name
// having resolved to root.
//
// Built with `locale.of("tr_TR")`; never by writing the struct literally, since a made-up handle is
// refused by the table rather than dereferenced.
pub struct Locale {
    handle: int
    name: string
}
